In opapātika and saṃsedaja beings, all five physical sensitivities arise simultaneously with the rebirth-consciousness. In the case of Brahmās, the bases for the nose, tongue, and body exist only as physical structures; they do not possess the sensitive matter (pasāda-dhātu).

Beings have past kamma that causes the eye to arise. Due to that kamma, kamma-born physical phenomena (kammaja-rūpa) arise. The sensitive matter that arises depending on those kamma-born great elements is called eye-sensitivity. Similarly, there is past kamma that causes the ear... (etc.) ...the nose, the tongue, and the body to arise. Due to that past kamma, kamma-born physical phenomena arise. The sensitive matters that arise depending on those kamma-born great elements are called ear-sensitivity, nose-sensitivity, tongue-sensitivity, and body-sensitivity. (This explains how the five physical sense bases arise due to the physical body).

Due to the mental body (nāma-kaya), which consists of mental factors such as Phassa, Vedanā, and Cetanā, the mind-sensitivity called manāyatana arises. This mind-sensitivity or manāyatana is indeed the consciousness (viññāṇa) which acts as the presiding lord. If that is so, one might ask: "It was stated earlier that 'consciousness causes name-and-form,' like a mother giving birth to children. Now, is it not like saying 'the children give birth to the mother'?" The answer is: A tree grows from a seed, and from the tree, a seed is produced again. In this process, the former seed is one, and the later seed is another. Similarly, among the 52 mental factors (cetasikas), there are times when Vitakka (initial application) and Vicāra (sustained application) are dominant; times when Viriya (effort) is dominant; times when Pīti (rapture) or Chanda (desire) is dominant; and times when Greed (lobha) is dominant, like a thirsty ox rushing to water;
